<interrogationes unitas="canon">

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I check an STML document against its schema from C?
    <responsum symbolum="canon_legere stml_legere canon_iudicare canon_nuntius CanonVitium">Load
      the schema with canon_legere(fons, piscina, intern, &amp;causa),
      parse the document with stml_legere (canon_examen shares one
      internamentum for both; plain canon_iudicare compares names by
      content, but the _expansum and _distributum variants need the
      tree's own), and call canon_iudicare(canon, r.radix, piscina).
      It returns a Xar of CanonVitium, and an empty Xar means the
      document is sound. Print each with canon_nuntius(v-&gt;genus) plus
      v-&gt;elementum and v-&gt;detail (chorda pointers that may be NIHIL);
      the line is v-&gt;nodus-&gt;linea when v-&gt;nodus is not NIHIL. A NIHIL
      canon or piscina gives NIHIL, not an empty Xar.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I check a .stml or .canon file against its canon from the shell?
    <responsum imperium="bin/canon_examen -machina canon.canon" via="tools/canon_examen.c canones.registrum">Run
      bin/canon_examen FILE... (the canon is looked up in
      canones.registrum) or bin/canon_examen -canon X.canon FILE; add
      -machina for TSV output. Run it from the repository root: it
      reads canones.registrum from the current directory, and -canon
      applies to the files named after it. Exit 0 = sound, 1 = vitia
      (a file that does not parse also counts), 2 = no file at all was
      judged. A file no registry line covers is only reported on
      stderr and skipped, so with other files judged the exit can
      still be 0. A .canon file is itself judged against canon.canon
      (registry keys &lt;canon&gt; and .canon), which is how typos inside
      a canon are caught.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I find which canon applies to a file?
    <responsum symbolum="canon_registrum_quaerere_radice canon_registrum_quaerere" via="canones.registrum tools/canon_examen.c">Read
      canones.registrum yourself and pass its text as catalogus: the
      library does no I/O. The registry has two kinds of key,
      '&lt;radix&gt;' (the document's root element) and '.ext' (the
      extension, taken from the last '.' anywhere in the path). Ask
      canon_registrum_quaerere_radice first and fall back to
      canon_registrum_quaerere only when it returns an empty chorda:
      the root wins because .stml carries several dialects. The library
      does not combine the two for you (canon_examen does). The answer
      is a slice of the catalogue text (not a piscina copy, whatever
      the header says), not NUL-terminated and alive only as long as
      the catalogue, so use chorda_ut_cstr before opening it.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what does a canon check, and what does it deliberately not check?
    <responsum symbolum="canon_iudicare CanonVitiumGenus" via="lib/canon.worklog.md">It
      checks a closed vocabulary (unknown elements and attributes are
      vitia), child counts per element (minimum, maximum, order
      ignored), attribute value genera, required attributes, where
      text is allowed, uniqueness (unicitas) and key references
      (citatio). It does not check co-occurrence rules ("a or b",
      "a &lt;= b"), inheritance, or references into other files except
      through a claves-externae block. There is no expression language
      on purpose: that road turns a schema into a programming language,
      as XSD did. Those rules belong to the caller.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I find out why canon_legere returned NIHIL?
    <responsum symbolum="canon_legere canon_ex_nodo">Pass
      a chorda* causa: on failure it holds the reason, for example the
      text does not parse ("canon parsari nequit"), the root is not
      &lt;canon&gt;, a citatio ad= has no '/', a unicitas or
      citatio names an attribute no element declares ("porta quae
      nihil custodit"), an ordinarius= default that does not fit its
      own genus or fines, fines on a non-numerus attribute, inverted
      or malformed fines, or a broken fragment or transclusion. Be careful the other way too: the
      reader tolerates unknown elements and attributes in a canon, so a
      misspelt keyword silently weakens it. Judge every .canon with
      canon.canon.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I validate a document that carries its own embedded canon?
    <responsum symbolum="canon_infixum_invenire canon_ex_nodo canon_iudicare" via="tools/canon_examen.c tools/silex.c">An
      embedded canon counts only as the FIRST element child of the root,
      named canon. canon_iudicare with the registry canon just treats
      it as invisible; it does not apply it. To honour it do what
      canon_examen does: canon_infixum_invenire(root), judge that node
      against canon.canon, build it with canon_ex_nodo, and judge the
      document a second time with it. Both judgments must pass (the
      registry canon is a floor the document cannot remove; the
      embedded one can only add rules). Not every tool does this:
      tools/silex.c judges with the embedded canon INSTEAD of the
      registry one and skips the canon.canon check.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I validate a document that uses templates, fragments or distribution wrappers?
    <responsum symbolum="canon_iudicare canon_iudicare_expansum canon_iudicare_distributum stml_expandere">canon_iudicare
      judges the file as written: fragments and transclusions are
      resolved transparently, but template calls are quoted material
      it does not see into. canon_iudicare_expansum runs stml_expandere
      first and judges what the calls produce;
      canon_iudicare_distributum also runs stml_distribuere, so it
      judges what a consumer finally sees. Both must get the tree's own
      internamentum (identities are compared by interned pointer, and a
      foreign one makes every call unknown). A failed expansion gives
      one CANON_EXPANSIO_FRACTA with nodus NIHIL, numerus =
      StmlExpansioVitium and limes = line.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what attribute value genera can a canon declare?
    <responsum symbolum="CanonGenusValoris" via="canon.canon">textus
      (anything), nomen ([a-zA-Z0-9_*]), compositum (nomen plus '-'),
      titulus (compositum with one optional leading dot), numerus
      (optional sign and digits; fractio="verum" allows a decimal point
      with digits on both sides; minimum=/maximum= are inclusive
      bounds compared digit by digit after stripping leading and
      trailing zeros, never converted to floats), veritas (verum or
      falsum), dies, electio (one of the &lt;optio&gt; children),
      identitas (&amp;name;) and referentia (&amp;name; or .genus). The
      reader takes an unknown genus word as textus without complaint;
      only judging the .canon against canon.canon (where genus= is an
      electio) catches the typo.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can a dies attribute reject impossible dates like 2026-02-30?
    <responsum symbolum="CanonGenusValoris canon_iudicare">No.
      dies accepts yyyy, yyyy-mm and yyyy-mm-dd and checks only the
      month (1-12) and the day (1-31), so 2026-02-30 passes. A leading
      '-' writes a year before Christ in the historical sense ("-312"
      is 312 BC, not the astronomical year -312), with I to IV digits
      for a bare year. Year zero is not rejected either: "0000" and
      "-0" pass although the source comment says that reading has no
      year zero.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can a unicitas rule use super="*" or the parent/child form like citatio does?
    <responsum symbolum="canon_iudicare canon_legere">No,
      and nothing warns you. citatio super= understands '*' and
      'parens/titulus', but unicitas compares each super= entry with
      element titles literally. The loader accepts both forms, because
      its check that the attribute exists understands them, so such a
      rule loads cleanly and then never matches a node: duplicates pass
      silently. Write plain element titles in unicitas super=.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I get typed C structs and a reader generated from a canon?
    <responsum imperium="tools/canon_coquere.sh -probare" via="tools/canon_coquere.c cocta.registrum">Add
      a line to cocta.registrum (canon, prefix, header, source) and
      run tools/canon_coquere.sh; -probare only checks that the
      committed readers are fresh. bin/canon_coquere turns each element
      into a struct (chorda* for textual genera, s64 for numerus, b32
      for veritas, each with a presence flag, an enum for electio, a
      pointer for a child of maximum 1, Xar* for unbounded children)
      plus a _legere_nodum reader. The generated reader applies
      ordinarius= defaults; canon_iudicare never does, since the judge
      only states the default.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

</interrogationes>
